<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  PERMIT CLEARTEXT TO LOOPBACK, IN THE DEBUG BUILD ONLY.

  Run 36770334063, and the reason b2 and a8 could not reach the test server is
  worth writing down, because it is a false pass hiding inside two real passes:

      Matrix b2 OFF/up -> — Offline — cannot reach GS. —
      Matrix b2 server saw 0 message request(s)
      Classify: mid-stream cut -> — Offline — cannot reach GS. —
      Classify: server saw 0 request(s)

  The server was never contacted. Not one `WireServer[...] <- POST .../messages`
  line appears in the log, and the request counter is zero.

  THE CAUSE. `targetSdk = 35` and NO `usesCleartextTraffic` and no network security
  config, so Android's default applies: cleartext is BLOCKED from API 28 onward.
  Every request to `http://127.0.0.1:<port>` is refused by policy before a socket
  is opened. WireServer was answering nothing because it was never asked anything.

  WHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN THE TWO SKIPS. b1, b3 and a8's refused-connect case
  still PASSED, and they would have passed with the server deleted. The tests say
  "a dead port produces the offline label", and what they were really measuring was
  "a cleartext request produces a network failure". Those coincide today, so the
  tests are not wrong in their conclusion, but they are not testing what they
  claim, and a
  mid-stream cut was being asserted against a server that was never reached, which
  is the same defect as a8 measuring its own harness for the fourth time.

  So the fix is to make loopback cleartext actually work in the debug build, which
  is what an instrumented test of a network path requires. Release is untouched:
  this file is under src/debug, and a release APK keeps the platform default of
  blocking cleartext, which is correct for a shipping app.

  NOTE FOR THE NEXT PERSON EDITING THIS: XML forbids a doubled hyphen inside a
  comment. Writing an em dash as two hyphens therefore makes the whole file
  unparseable, and the error points at a column number and never mentions a
  comment. That has now happened twice in this repository, in two different
  manifests, and the second time it happened inside the very note warning about
  it. Use a comma, a semicolon, or a real dash character.

  The scope is loopback and the emulator's host alias only. This is not a blanket
  `usesCleartextTraffic="true"`, which would permit cleartext to any host and
  would be a real weakness if it ever reached a release build.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <!-- The host machine as seen from the Android emulator. -->
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">10.0.2.2</domain>
        <!-- Loopback, which is where the test server binds. -->
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">127.0.0.1</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="false">localhost</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
